## Final revision decisions for implementation

The user has approved the twelve decisions below and the four feeder passages under [Transfer-network sub-analysis](#transfer-network-sub-analysis-25-november-2026), including the Discussion paragraph and its two sources. Start from the current working files under `pnas_report/paper_simple` and preserve the existing local changes. The earlier ZIPs are review snapshots. Use the approved wording below and the [implementation and build checks](#implementation-and-build-checks) for the new build.

### 1. Abstract entry-rate wording

**Confirmed.** In [abstract.tex](../abstract.tex), replace the sentence beginning “We analysed 16,636 eligible transfers” with:

> We analysed 16,636 eligible transfers in recorded seasons 2012/13–2024/25 as a directed network and compared Premier League entry rates (ages 30+ and under 30).

Use the manuscript's LaTeX season-range formatting. The comparison uses the share of all eligible transfers in each age group that ended at a Premier League club. This edit preserves the abstract word count.

### 2. Abstract sentiment model types

**User-supplied wording.** In [abstract.tex](../abstract.tex), replace the sentence beginning “Sentiment analysis retained 820” with:

> Sentiment analysis retained 820 sufficiently covered signings and evaluated four models (contextual and lexicon-based) against 196 human-labelled sentences.

Keep “Sentiment analysis” explicit. RoBERTa is the contextual model. VADER and the two Bing variants are lexicon-based. This adds three words. Allow for a three-word reduction elsewhere in the final 150-word abstract while preserving the two agreed sentences.

### 3. Introduction before-and-after tone comparison and validation

**Confirmed.** In [introduction.tex](../introduction.tex), keep the research question and transfer-network sentence. Replace the sentence beginning “In a separate sample” with:

> In a separate sample of selected Premier League signings, we compare Guardian sentence sentiment before and after each move to ask whether coverage tone changes differently for older and younger players.

Follow it with the confirmed validation wording:

> To assess the performance of the sentiment models, we compared their scores with manual labels for the same Guardian sentences. One author judged each sentence in the comparison as positive, negative or neutral, providing a human reference for the evaluation.

### 4. Introduction corpus and token analysis

**Confirmed.** In [introduction.tex](../introduction.tex), replace the final two sentences beginning “Article counts provide context” with:

> The selected sentences from these articles form the corpus for the sentiment analysis. To describe its vocabulary, we tokenize the sentences and examine which tokens occur most often and how their frequencies vary by rank.

### 5. Transfer-network modularity comparison

**Confirmed.** In [results.tex](../results.tex), replace the last sentence of the Leiden paragraph under “Transfer-network structure” with:

> Both methods identified five communities in the observed network, with broadly similar club memberships. Modularity was higher in the observed network than in the same randomized network for both Leiden (0.448 versus 0.215) and Louvain (0.446 versus 0.213). By this measure, transfer activity was more strongly concentrated within communities in the observed network.

Add the following after the Louvain and Leiden setup under “Transfer network and backbone” in [methods.tex](../methods.tex):

> We generated one randomized network by rewiring routes while preserving each club’s number of incoming and outgoing routes, then randomly reassigning the original route weights. We ran both methods on this same network.

This is a descriptive comparison against one randomized network. It uses directed, transfer-count-weighted modularity. [Saved restricted comparison](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/transfer_community_comparison/method_comparison.md)

### 6. Vocabulary paragraph corpus terminology

**User-supplied wording.** In [results.tex](../results.tex), replace the final sentence of the vocabulary paragraph under “Measuring tone in football writing” with:

> These counts partly describe the vocabulary of the selected corpus. The following analysis examines the tone of the sentences.

### 7. Spearman’s rank correlation in the validation Results

**Confirmed.** In [results.tex](../results.tex), replace the sentence beginning “Spearman’s ρ measures” in the validation paragraph with:

> The human labels were ordered from negative to neutral to positive, with all sentences in each category sharing a rank. Spearman’s ρ compares those category ranks with the ranks of each model’s numerical scores. A positive ρ indicates that the model’s ranking tended to agree with the order of the three categories.

Typeset the coefficient as `$\rho$`. The human labels were categorical and the rank comparison uses their order. A read-only check of the 196 saved paired labels and scores reproduced RoBERTa’s coefficient (0.5158) and 95% percentile bootstrap interval (0.3839–0.6314), along with VADER (0.3322) and the two Bing variants (0.3562 and 0.3638). Retain the reference to the human-validation table (Table 2 in the current draft) and the reported RoBERTa values. [Validation code](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/b9_score.py#L57) · [Saved scores](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/b9_model_scores.csv) · [SciPy definition](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.spearmanr.html)

### 8. Closing RoBERTa sentence in the validation Results

**Confirmed.** Delete the final sentence of the validation paragraph in [results.tex](../results.tex), without replacement:

> We therefore used RoBERTa as the main instrument and retained the other methods for comparison.

The preceding discussion of age-specific comparability remains the closing sentence. The following Results subsection reports the RoBERTa age comparison and summarizes VADER and the two Bing variants.

### 9. Tone-change calculation and age-label permutation

**Confirmed.** In [results.tex](../results.tex) under “Changes in Guardian tone by age,” replace the passage from “To test whether tone changed differently by age” through its RoBERTa interpretation with:

> First, we calculated one tone change per signing by subtracting its mean sentiment score before the move from its mean score after the move. We averaged these changes within each age group, giving every signing equal weight. With RoBERTa, the mean change was −0.0076 for the 30+ group and −0.0137 for the under-30 group. Subtracting the younger group’s mean change from the older group’s, using unrounded values, gave a gap of +0.0060 score units.
>
> We kept each signing’s tone change fixed and shuffled the age-group labels within each season 5,000 times, recalculating the gap after each shuffle. We found no clear evidence that sentiment changed differently for older and younger signings.

Keep the following sentence about VADER and both Bing variants. Omit the numerical p-value from this Results passage. Keep it in the Abstract and Figure 3 caption. The full test specification remains in Methods. [Permutation code](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_age30.py#L41) · [Saved results](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/a1_age30_results.csv)

### 10. Discussion of coverage, prominence and quoted people

**Confirmed.** In [discussion.tex](../discussion.tex), replace the sentence beginning “Player prominence and the mix” with:

> Player prominence may influence which signings receive coverage and which performances or topics get attention. Sentiment scores may also reflect praise or criticism from people quoted in the articles, such as players, coaches or commentators. The contribution of each of these factors remains uncertain.

### 11. Discussion of currency tokens and sentiment

**Confirmed.** In [discussion.tex](../discussion.tex), add the following after the frequency-pattern and Zipf discussion, before the sentence beginning “RoBERTa scores the whole sentence”:

> The pound sign (£) was the second most frequent token after stopword removal (Table 1). The language around currency amounts may influence sentiment scores, for example when a transfer fee is described as a bargain or as excessive. The influence of currency symbols and the surrounding language on the scores remains untested.

Use the native LaTeX pound symbol command and `Table~\ref{tab:guardian-tokens}`. The rank refers to the stopword-filtered column. [Token table source](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/guardian_text_description_findings/top_ten_words.md)

### 12. Figure 1 community names

**Confirmed.** In [figure_floats.tex](../figure_floats.tex), insert this sentence in Figure 1(a)'s caption immediately after “Colours and shapes identify five Louvain communities”:

> Each community is named after its member club with the highest total degree before disparity filtering.

A check of the saved memberships reproduced all five labels by maximizing unweighted in-degree plus out-degree among members on the full graph. The selection code breaks ties by smaller club ID. The disparity filter at $\alpha = 0.20$ selects the routes displayed in the figure. [Representative selection](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/notebooks/transfer_community_outputs.py#L51) · [Saved summary](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/louvain_transfer_communities_findings/community_summary.csv)

