## Transfer-network sub-analysis, 25 November 2026

The user corrected the figure and summary links to the 2012/13–2024/25 rerun. Figure 4 uses the linked [feeder PDF](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/premier_league_feeder_findings_three_panel.pdf). [Results](../results.tex#L54) describes the 217 screened routes among 2,396 Premier League arrivals, the Girona to Manchester City example (15 observed transfers and 1.8 expected), and the median transfer counts of four and two for the joint-ownership and remaining routes. [Methods](../methods.tex#L44) describes the 16,636-transfer source sample, the destination shuffle, the unadjusted screen over 11,448 pairs and the ownership grouping. The Figure 4 caption distinguishes the two manually included routes from the documented transfer-date overlaps. [Restricted feeder analysis summary](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/premier_league_feeder_summary.md)

The [local draft](../output/pdf/issue14_feeder_layout/main.pdf) fits six pages after tightening plot margins and placing references in the last text column. The new Results paragraph is on page 4, Figure 4 is on page 5 and the new Methods subsection is on page 6. The final Overleaf package must include the feeder PDF and use a project-local figure path in `results.tex`. The Guardian corpus prose review below remains pending before final compilation.

### Feeder wording review for the implementation handoff

**Results approved.** Keep the heading “Sub-analysis of the transfer network.” Replace its prose in [results.tex](../results.tex#L54) with the user's draft and the three accepted edits:

> During the network analysis, we also examined which club pairs accounted for unusually many transfers among the 2,396 Premier League arrivals. The exploratory screen selected 217 recurring routes, each with at least two recorded transfers, accounting for 626 transfers (Figure 4). Girona to Manchester City was the busiest transfer pair, with 15 recorded transfers over the study period. We calculated an expected count for that route in each season, keeping fixed how many transfers each origin club sent to the Premier League and how many each destination club received. Adding those seasonal expectations gave an expected total of 1.8 transfers across the full 2012/13–2024/25 period.
>
> We found that some high-volume transfer pairs shared a common equity investor. The 17 transfer pairs in the joint-ownership group had a median of four transfers. In comparison, the remaining 200 transfer pairs had a median of two (Figure 4b).

Use the existing `fig:feeder-routes` reference for Figure 4 and append panel (b) to the second reference. The 1.8 expected transfers are a pooled count, calculated by summing seasonal expectations. [Restricted feeder summary](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/premier_league_feeder_summary.md)

**Figure 4 caption.** The user directed removal of the separate sentence about the Grasshoppers and Vitesse fractions and confirmed that these pairs had a joint ownership interest. The caption uses the prior draft with that deletion:

> **Recurring transfer routes into the Premier League.** **(a)** The six screened transfer pairs with at least seven recorded transfers among 2,396 arrivals in recorded seasons 2012/13–2024/25. Bars show observed counts and diamonds show expectations under the seasonal destination shuffle. For Girona and Udinese, the red fractions show how many of each route’s transfers occurred during documented joint ownership. **(b)** The exceedance curves show the percentage of routes in each group with at least a given number of recorded transfers.

Use this text in the Figure 4 caption in [results.tex](../results.tex#L61). The explanation of transfer-date fractions applies to Girona and Udinese. Keep the exceedance threshold inclusive.

**Methods approved.** Keep the heading “Recurring routes into the Premier League.” Replace its paragraph in [methods.tex](../methods.tex#L44) with:

> We counted 2,396 Premier League arrivals from the 16,636 eligible transfers in recorded seasons 2012/13–2024/25 by directed club pair. We shuffled destinations 999 times within each season (seed 2026), preserving each origin and destination total. We retained routes with at least two recorded transfers and an unadjusted upper-tail permutation p < 0.05. The joint-ownership group included transfer pairs whose clubs shared an equity investor, plus two pairs included on the basis of family or financial ties.

Use the manuscript's LaTeX notation for the season range and p-value threshold. The wording describes the grouping in the restricted analysis. [Ownership audit](../../notes/premier_league_feeder_ownership_audit.md#unresolved-and-excluded-network-relationships)

### Feeder financial context in the Discussion

**Confirmed.** Insert the following after the discussion of Premier League entry and before the Guardian limitations in [discussion.tex](../discussion.tex). The user explicitly requested LaTeX citations.

```latex
Transfer fees between related clubs can move funds within an ownership group. A fee above market value could increase the selling club's reported profit before regulatory adjustments, and UEFA's financial rules limit the proceeds clubs can count from permanent sales to related clubs \cite{uefa2024financialsustainability}. In UEFA's separate review of eligibility for the 2024/25 Champions League, Girona and Manchester City, the clubs on our busiest route, agreed to temporary restrictions on transfers between them \cite{uefa2024multiclubownership}. The route counts give a starting point for testing whether fees differ from those for comparable players moving between independently owned clubs.
```

Add the matching entries to [references.bib](../references.bib).

```bibtex
@misc{uefa2024financialsustainability,
  author       = {{UEFA}},
  title        = {{UEFA} club licensing and financial sustainability regulations},
  year         = {2024},
  howpublished = {2024 edition, Annex G.3.7},
  url          = {https://www.fsf.fo/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/20240601_Regulations_CLFS_2024_EN_22-10-2024.pdf},
  note         = {\href{https://www.fsf.fo/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/20240601_Regulations_CLFS_2024_EN_22-10-2024.pdf}{Regulations}}
}

@misc{uefa2024multiclubownership,
  author = {{UEFA}},
  title  = {The {CFCB} decides on multi-club ownership cases for the 2024/25 {UEFA} club competitions},
  year   = {2024},
  month  = jul,
  url    = {https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/028f-1b4ba6fcea09-078845f25cbf-1000--/},
  note   = {\href{https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/028f-1b4ba6fcea09-078845f25cbf-1000--/}{5 July decision}}
}
```

Annex G.3.7 addresses accounting for permanent related-party transfers. The eligibility decision documents the clubs' commitments to demonstrate independence and includes an exception for pre-existing agreements. The feeder analysis measured route counts and leaves fee levels and accounting effects untested. [Research notes](issue14_feeder_financial_context.md)

